Use the real Micro Eazy mark, and pin the installed name

The app drew its own logo: a CSS letter "M" in a rounded box, in the rail
wordmark and again on the Welcome band. The brand mark is a navy-and-green M
with a rising arrow and a KES coin, and it was already sitting unused in
public/brand/micro-eazy/. Both now render icon-192.png — the same file the
manifest installs to the home screen, so the icon a customer taps and the mark
at the top of the app are one image rather than two things that resemble each
other.

On the white chip, which is the treatment the previous app already settled
(pwa/src/components/eco/EazyLoader.jsx: "the app icon, on the white chip"). The
mark is navy on transparency, so on the navy Welcome band and on the near-black
dark-mode rail its navy half would sink into the ground and the logo would read
as a green smear. A launcher gives it a solid plate anyway, so this is also what
the customer already recognises. Verified rendered in both themes.

The installed name is pinned to exactly "Micro Eazy" via
apple-mobile-web-app-title. The manifest name and short_name already said so and
that is what Android reads, but iOS names a home-screen shortcut from that meta
tag and falls back to the <title> when it is absent — which would have labelled
the icon "Micro Eazy — Quick loans. Bet…", a truncated sentence under an app
icon. The title keeps its tagline for the browser tab; the home screen does not.

